<p class="text-large"><a href="/the-nogood-book/">The NoGood book</a> is finally done. It took nearly two years, and I still can't quite believe it. When my 400 copies arrived from the printer, I was nervous as hell. But when I opened one of the many boxes stacked in my studio, took out a book and flipped through its pages, the worries disappeared. This project turned out exactly the way I intended.</p>

<div class="video fade-in">
    <video autoplay loop muted playsinline src="/assets/img/book/NG_Unboxing.mp4"></video>
</div>

<p class="text-large">I planned to write more updates during production, but the work—on the book, and on other projects—took all my time and energy. Now that the book is out and pre-orders have landed on my backers' doormats, here's a look back at the last six months.</p>

<h3 class="uppercase pixel-font">Home stretch</h3>
<p class="text-large">With each round of changes, and every passing week spent chipping away at the design, the book's shape became clearer. By the start of 2026 it was mostly locked in. The crowdfunding campaign had passed €5,000, which let me change the size of the book so I could display the illustrations in a larger format. All the articles were in, all my sketches collected. What was left was production.</p>
<p class="text-large">The first quarter of 2026 was one long sprint. I drew the illustrations for the articles in the Context section, finalised the design, spine and cover, moved the illustrations around until they sat on the right pages, and finished the writing with <a href="https://daniel.pizza" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daniël</a>—hiring him to write the book was handing off the one thing I couldn't do myself.</p>

<div class="slideshow slideshow-2 fade-in">
    {% image "source/assets/img/book/NG_Proof.jpg", "The NoGood Book proof" %}
    {% image "source/assets/img/book/NG_Proof_02.jpg", "The NoGood Book proof" %}
</div>

<p class="text-large">Then the tedious bit: pre-processing every image and illustration. The printer sent a proof and, as I have a background in print design—I designed newspapers for a couple of years—I knew what I was looking at. But there's always guesswork involved. No matter how often you go over technical specifications, ink limits, colour profiles and gamut warnings, or how many times you brighten or darken an illustration: you never know exactly how the book will turn out. I prepared as best I could.</p>


<p class="text-large">I visited the printer a few weeks after submitting my files, taking my father along; he loves this kind of thing. The men operating the giant machinery explained how they worked and showed us the presses' inner workings. The pages flew through them faster than the eye could register. A surreal, beautiful experience.</p>

